5. Current Project Status
The project has successfully completed the machine learning pipeline, Flask backend development, frontend integration, prediction history logging, and Apache Spark analytics. Users can enter soil and environmental parameters through the web interface and receive real-time crop recommendations generated by the trained Gaussian Naive Bayes model.
Prediction history is automatically stored in a CSV file, and Apache Spark processes this data to generate analytical insights such as total predictions, most recommended crop, average temperature, average rainfall, and crop distribution.
Apache Hive integration is currently in progress and will be used to store and query prediction history using SQL-like operations as part of the Big Data component of the project.
6. Dataset Description
The Crop Recommendation Dataset is used to train the machine learning model. It contains information about soil nutrients and environmental conditions required for crop growth. The dataset consists of multiple instances representing different agricultural conditions and their corresponding recommended crops.
The input features used in this project are:
Feature	Description
Nitrogen (N)	Nitrogen content in the soil
Phosphorus (P)	Phosphorus content in the soil
Potassium (K)	Potassium content in the soil
Temperature	Average temperature (°C)
Humidity	Relative humidity (%)
pH	Soil pH value
Rainfall	Rainfall received (mm)


The target variable is the crop that is most suitable for cultivation under the given conditions.
Before training, the dataset was examined for missing values, duplicate records, and inconsistent data types. The feature names were standardized and the target labels were encoded into numerical values for machine learning.
7. System Architecture
The Crop Recommendation System follows a three-layer architecture consisting of the presentation layer, application layer, and data processing layer.
The Presentation Layer provides a web interface where users enter soil and environmental parameters.
The Application Layer is implemented using Flask. It receives user input, validates the data, sends it to the trained machine learning model, and returns the predicted crop.
The Data Processing Layer consists of the trained Gaussian Naive Bayes model, prediction history storage, and Apache Spark analytics. Each prediction is stored in a CSV file, which is later analyzed using Apache Spark to generate statistical insights. Apache Hive integration is planned to provide SQL-based querying over prediction history.
8. System Workflow
8. System Workflow
The workflow of the proposed system consists of the following steps:
1. The user enters soil nutrient and weather parameters through the web interface.
2. The frontend sends the input data to the Flask backend using a REST API.
3. The backend preprocesses the input and passes it to the trained machine learning model.
4. The model predicts the most suitable crop.
5. The predicted crop is displayed to the user.
6. The prediction is stored in the prediction history CSV file.
7. Apache Spark reads the prediction history and generates analytical statistics.
8. Apache Hive (under integration) will provide SQL-based querying and reporting on the stored prediction history.
9. Machine Learning Methodology
9. Machine Learning Methodology
Several supervised machine learning algorithms were evaluated to determine the most suitable model for crop prediction.
The following algorithms were compared:
- Logistic Regression
- Decision Tree
- Random Forest
- K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)
- Gaussian Naive Bayes
The models were trained using an 80:20 train-test split and evaluated based on prediction accuracy.
Among the evaluated models, Gaussian Naive Bayes achieved the highest performance while providing fast prediction speed and low computational complexity. Therefore, it was selected as the final model and exported using the Joblib library for deployment within the Flask application.
10. Apache Spark Integration
10. Apache Spark Integration
Apache Spark is integrated into the system to perform analytical processing on the prediction history generated by the application.
Every prediction made by the machine learning model is stored in a CSV file. Spark reads this file and performs distributed data processing operations to generate useful analytical information.
The Spark analytics module provides the following statistics:
- Total number of predictions
- Most frequently recommended crop
- Recommendation count
- Average temperature
- Average rainfall
- Crop distribution
The analytics results are exposed through a REST API endpoint, allowing users or developers to retrieve statistical information in JSON format.
11. Implementation
11. Implementation
The system is implemented using a modular architecture consisting of frontend, backend, machine learning, and big data components.
The frontend is developed using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. It provides an interface for users to enter soil nutrient values and environmental parameters.
The backend is developed using the Flask framework in Python. It receives input from the frontend through REST APIs, validates the data, and forwards it to the trained machine learning model for prediction.
The machine learning component uses a Gaussian Naive Bayes classifier trained on the Crop Recommendation Dataset. The trained model is serialized using the Joblib library and loaded by the Flask application during startup.
Each prediction generated by the application is automatically stored in a CSV file named prediction_history.csv. Apache Spark processes this file to generate analytical information such as prediction count, crop distribution, average rainfall, and average temperature.
The system follows a layered architecture that separates presentation, business logic, machine learning, and analytics components, making the application easier to maintain and extend.